  "body": {
    "type": "doc",
    "content": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Nam prik ong serves 4 as a snack or starter and takes about 30 minutes. The dip is built from "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "pork mince",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "glossaryTooltip",
                "attrs": {
                  "termSlug": "pork-mince"
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " cooked with "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "shrimp paste",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "glossaryTooltip",
                "attrs": {
                  "termSlug": "shrimp-paste"
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": ", "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "tinned tomatoes",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "glossaryTooltip",
                "attrs": {
                  "termSlug": "tinned-tomatoes"
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": ", and aromatics. It is served warm with an assortment of raw and blanched vegetables. Crunchy beansprouts, cucumber slices, and cabbage wedges are all good dipping vehicles."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Fry the shrimp paste in the oil for 30 seconds before adding anything else. This step removes the raw edge and builds the savoury base of the dip."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
